<file path="doc/goal/implementation/02_VALIDATOR_AND_TOOLCHAIN.md">
  1: # Stage D — Toolchain and Static Validation
  2: 
  3: ## Objective
  4: 
  5: Provide deterministic TypeScript CLI tools that share the pure puzzle domain and reject malformed level data before solving or generation promotion.
  6: 
  7: ## CLI contracts
  8: 
  9: ### `npm run levels:validate`
 10: 
 11: Input defaults to the full runtime catalog. Optional arguments may select an ID range, but full-catalog behavior is mandatory.
 12: 
 13: Exit codes:
 14: 
 15: - `0`: all selected levels valid;
 16: - `1`: one or more validation errors;
 17: - `2`: tool/configuration/runtime failure.
 18: 
 19: Human output must include level ID, issue code, entity IDs, and cell/path context.
 20: 
 21: Machine output must be written to:
 22: 
 23: ```text
 24: reports/levels/validation.json
 25: ```
 26: 
 27: ## Structured issue model
 28: 
 29: ```ts
 30: export type ValidationSeverity = "error" | "warning";
 31: 
 32: export interface ValidationIssue {
 33:   severity: ValidationSeverity;
 34:   code: ValidationIssueCode;
 35:   levelId: LevelId;
 36:   message: string;
 37:   entityIds: EntityId[];
 38:   cells: Cell[];
 39:   path: string;
 40: }
 41: 
 42: export interface ValidationResult {
 43:   valid: boolean;
 44:   issues: ValidationIssue[];
 45: }
 46: ```
 47: 
 48: Sort issues by level ID, severity, code, path, and entity IDs.
 49: 
 50: ## Required error codes
 51: 
 52: ```text
 53: LEVEL_ID_INVALID
 54: LEVEL_ID_DUPLICATE
 55: LEVEL_PHASE_MISMATCH
 56: BOARD_DIMENSION_INVALID
 57: LIVES_INVALID
 58: TIME_LIMIT_INVALID
 59: ROTATE_CHARGES_INVALID
 60: ENTITY_ID_DUPLICATE
 61: ENTITY_TYPE_INVALID
 62: GEOMETRY_NOT_INTEGER
 63: FOOTPRINT_NON_POSITIVE
 64: FOOTPRINT_OUT_OF_BOUNDS
 65: PHYSICAL_FOOTPRINT_OVERLAP
 66: SWITCH_PHYSICAL_OVERLAP
 67: UNIT_DIRECTION_INVALID
 68: UNIT_RUNTIME_STATE_INVALID
 69: GATE_OPEN_INVALID
 70: SWITCH_ACTIVATED_INVALID
 71: SWITCH_TARGET_MISSING
 72: SWITCH_TARGET_NOT_GATE
 73: ASSET_KEY_EMPTY
 74: CATALOG_SIZE_INVALID
 75: CATALOG_ORDER_INVALID
 76: MANIFEST_MISSING
 77: MANIFEST_LEVEL_MISMATCH
 78: ```
 79: 
 80: ## Physical overlap rules
 81: 
 82: Initial static footprints of live units, obstacles, and closed gates are mutually exclusive. Switch footprints must not overlap those physical footprints even though switches do not block forward movement at runtime.
 83: 
 84: Open gates in authored initial data are allowed only when a phase manifest explicitly justifies them; otherwise emit a warning or error according to the phase contract. The default catalog should start gates closed unless the lesson explicitly requires otherwise.
 85: 
 86: ## Entity field rules
 87: 
 88: - Unit: direction required; open/targetId/activated forbidden.
 89: - Obstacle: direction/escaped/open/targetId/activated forbidden.
 90: - Gate: open required; direction/escaped/targetId/activated forbidden.
 91: - Switch: targetId and activated required; direction/escaped/open forbidden.
 92: 
 93: If normalized runtime types make forbidden fields impossible at compile time, the validator still checks raw authoring input before normalization.
 94: 
 95: ## Catalog validation
 96: 
 97: The full catalog must contain exactly 100 IDs in this order:
 98: 
 99: ```text
100: level-001 ... level-100
101: ```
102: 
103: Phase mapping is fixed:
104: 
105: - 001–020: phase 1
106: - 021–040: phase 2
107: - 041–060: phase 3
108: - 061–080: phase 4
109: - 081–100: phase 5
110: 
111: ## Manifest validation
112: 
113: Every level must have an authoring manifest with:
114: 
115: ```ts
116: export interface LevelAuthoringManifest {
117:   levelId: LevelId;
118:   phase: DifficultyPhase;
119:   sourceKind: "authored" | "generated" | "generated-with-override";
120:   generatorVersion: string;
121:   seed: string | null;
122:   templateId: string;
123:   concepts: string[];
124:   intendedOpening: PuzzleAction[];
125:   rotateRequired: boolean;
126:   intentionalDeadEnd: boolean;
127:   deadEndSignal: string | null;
128:   targetBand: DifficultyTargetBand;
129:   approvedExceptions: ApprovedMetricException[];
130: }
131: ```
132: 
133: No target exception may be implicit. It requires a reason and affected metric.
134: 
135: ## Required validator tests
136: 
137: - Level 5 overlap fixture reports exact cell and both unit IDs.
138: - Out-of-bounds horizontal and vertical multi-cell footprints.
139: - Duplicate level and entity IDs.
140: - Missing/incorrect switch target.
141: - Switch overlap with a physical entity.
142: - Illegal fields per entity type.
143: - Invalid phase and level ID order.
144: - Missing manifest.
145: - Valid gate/switch and multi-cell examples pass.
146: 
147: ## Promotion gate
148: 
149: Generation may not write `levels.ts` when validation has any error. Warnings must appear in reports and may pass only if they are non-release-blocking by explicit code policy.
</file>

<file path="doc/goal/implementation/03_HYBRID_SOLVER.md">
  1: # Stage E — Hybrid Solver
  2: 
  3: ## Objective
  4: 
  5: Prove solvability with the same transition engine as runtime while avoiding exponential enumeration of independent exit orders.
  6: 
  7: ## Key design decision
  8: 
  9: Under v1 rules, exit actions are monotonic and pairwise commuting when both are legal in the same state. The solver therefore collapses exit permutations into a deterministic **exit closure**. Search branches only on rotate decisions.
 10: 
 11: This exit-closure mechanism is the required partial-order reduction for the released v1 rules.
 12: 
 13: ## Solver result
 14: 
 15: ```ts
 16: export type SolveStatus = "SOLVABLE" | "UNSOLVABLE" | "INCONCLUSIVE";
 17: export type SolverPath = "FAST_EXIT_CLOSURE" | "STATEFUL_ROTATE_SEARCH";
 18: 
 19: export interface SolveCost {
 20:   rotateCount: number;
 21:   totalActionCount: number;
 22: }
 23: 
 24: export interface SolverDiagnostics {
 25:   exploredStates: number;
 26:   generatedStates: number;
 27:   prunedDominatedStates: number;
 28:   collapsedExitActions: number;
 29:   frontierPeak: number;
 30:   elapsedMs: number;
 31:   stateBudget: number;
 32:   timeBudgetMs: number;
 33: }
 34: 
 35: export interface SolveResult {
 36:   levelId: LevelId;
 37:   status: SolveStatus;
 38:   solverPath: SolverPath;
 39:   cost: SolveCost | null;
 40:   actions: PuzzleAction[];
 41:   finalStateKey: string | null;
 42:   diagnostics: SolverDiagnostics;
 43:   reason: string | null;
 44: }
 45: ```
 46: 
 47: ## Deterministic exit closure
 48: 
 49: Create `scripts/levels/solver/exitClosure.ts`.
 50: 
 51: ```ts
 52: export interface ExitClosureResult {
 53:   state: PuzzleState;
 54:   actions: PuzzleAction[];
 55:   transitions: PuzzleTransitionResult[];
 56: }
 57: 
 58: export function applyDeterministicExitClosure(
 59:   level: TribeOutLevel,
 60:   initialState: PuzzleState,
 61: ): ExitClosureResult;
 62: ```
 63: 
 64: Algorithm:
 65: 
 66: ```text
 67: state := initialState
 68: trace := []
 69: loop:
 70:   legalExits := listLegalExitActions(level, state), sorted by unitId
 71:   if legalExits is empty: return state and trace
 72:   action := first legal exit
 73:   result := applyPuzzleAction(level, state, action)
 74:   assert result.accepted
 75:   state := result.nextState
 76:   append action and result
 77: ```
 78: 
 79: Completeness rationale:
 80: 
 81: - A successful exit cannot invalidate another currently legal exit.
 82: - It only removes occupancy or opens gates.
 83: - Therefore choosing a deterministic legal exit cannot destroy a no-rotate solution.
 84: - Repeating to closure solves every state solvable with exits alone.
 85: 
 86: Directly test the commutativity premise. Do not rely only on comments.
 87: 
 88: ## Fast path
 89: 
 90: `fastSolver.ts`:
 91: 
 92: 1. Create the initial puzzle state.
 93: 2. Apply deterministic exit closure.
 94: 3. If complete, return `SOLVABLE`, `FAST_EXIT_CLOSURE`, rotate count 0.
 95: 4. If not complete and no rotate charges remain, return `UNSOLVABLE`.
 96: 5. Otherwise return a handoff state for stateful search.
 97: 
 98: The 40-independent-unit fixture must complete through the fast path with explored states bounded by a small linear threshold, recommended `<= 2` search states plus 40 closure actions.
 99: 
100: ## Stateful path
101: 
102: Use lexicographic Dijkstra over **closed macro-states**.
103: 
104: Every queue node is normalized by applying exit closure before insertion. Therefore the queue never branches over individual exit orders.
105: 
106: ### Node fields
107: 
108: ```ts
109: interface SearchNode {
110:   state: PuzzleState;              // already exit-closed
111:   stateKey: string;
112:   rotateCount: number;
113:   totalActionCount: number;
114:   actions: PuzzleAction[];
115: }
116: ```
117: 
118: ### Priority
119: 
120: Compare in order:
121: 
122: 1. `rotateCount` ascending;
123: 2. `totalActionCount` ascending;
124: 3. `stateKey` ascending for deterministic ties.
125: 
126: The selected solution must prefer 0 rotates and 12 total actions over 1 rotate and 9 total actions.
127: 
128: ### Successor generation
129: 
130: For each legal rotate action sorted by unit ID:
131: 
132: 1. Apply the rotate through `applyPuzzleAction`.
133: 2. Apply deterministic exit closure to the rotated state.
134: 3. Concatenate rotate action and closure actions.
135: 4. Serialize the closed successor.
136: 5. Insert only if not dominated.
137: 
138: ### Dominance
139: 
140: For an identical canonical state key, retain only the best lexicographic cost. A worse or equal cost is pruned.
141: 
142: Because rotate charges are part of the state key, do not use an unsound rule that treats states with different remaining charges as identical.
143: 
144: ### Budgets
145: 
146: Default release budgets must be exported constants and written into reports. Recommended initial values:
147: 
148: ```text
149: stateBudget: 250,000 closed states per level
150: timeBudgetMs: 5,000 per level in CI
151: ```
152: 
153: The implementation may tune these values based on measured performance, but the final values must be explicit and all 100 release levels must resolve within them.
154: 
155: Budget exhaustion returns `INCONCLUSIVE`, never `UNSOLVABLE` or `SOLVABLE`.
156: 
157: ## Replay verification
158: 
159: Every `SOLVABLE` trace must be replayed from `createInitialPuzzleState` using `applyPuzzleAction`.
160: 
161: Acceptance requires:
162: 
163: - every action accepted;
164: - final state complete;
165: - replay final key equals solver final key;
166: - replay rotate count and action count equal reported cost.
167: 
168: ## CLI
169: 
170: `npm run levels:solve` writes:
171: 
172: ```text
173: reports/levels/solutions.json
174: reports/levels/solutions.md
175: ```
176: 
177: The command fails when any selected level is invalid, unsolvable, inconclusive, or has a non-replayable trace.
178: 
179: ## Mandatory fixtures
180: 
181: - simple monotonic chain;
182: - 40 independent units;
183: - permanent obstacle lock with zero rotate charges;
184: - one switch opening one gate;
185: - two-layer gate chain;
186: - one required rotate;
187: - wrong rotate dead end;
188: - competing zero-rotate/one-rotate solutions;
189: - multi-cell paths in all directions;
190: - tiny state budget returns `INCONCLUSIVE`;
191: - trace replay.
</file>

<file path="doc/goal/implementation/04_DIFFICULTY_ANALYZER.md">
  1: # Stage F — Difficulty Analyzer
  2: 
  3: ## Objective
  4: 
  5: Measure logical depth, strategic decisions, statefulness, and visual density separately. The analyzer must not mistake high unit count for deep puzzle design.
  6: 
  7: ## Report model
  8: 
  9: ```ts
 10: export type MetricCoverage = "exact" | "sampled" | "not-applicable";
 11: 
 12: export interface MetricValue<T> {
 13:   value: T;
 14:   coverage: MetricCoverage;
 15:   sampleCount: number | null;
 16:   seed: string | null;
 17: }
 18: 
 19: export interface DifficultyReport {
 20:   levelId: LevelId;
 21:   phase: DifficultyPhase;
 22:   unitCount: number;
 23:   obstacleCount: number;
 24:   gateCount: number;
 25:   switchCount: number;
 26:   multiCellUnitCount: number;
 27:   initialAvailableMoves: number;
 28:   initialAvailableRatio: number;
 29:   criticalPathLength: MetricValue<number | null>;
 30:   causalUnlockDepth: MetricValue<number>;
 31:   averageAvailableMoves: MetricValue<number>;
 32:   forcedStateRatio: MetricValue<number>;
 33:   minRotateRequired: number;
 34:   deadEndRisk: MetricValue<number>;
 35:   meaningfulDecisionProxy: MetricValue<number>;
 36:   mechanicUsage: MechanicUsageReport;
 37:   phaseTargetStatus: "IN_BAND" | "OUT_OF_BAND" | "APPROVED_EXCEPTION";
 38:   outOfBandReasons: string[];
 39: }
 40: ```
 41: 
 42: ## Metrics
 43: 
 44: ### `initialAvailableMoves`
 45: 
 46: Count legal exit actions in the initial puzzle state. Do not count rotate actions.
 47: 
 48: ### `initialAvailableRatio`
 49: 
 50: ```text
 51: initialAvailableMoves / unitCount
 52: ```
 53: 
 54: Return 0 for invalid zero-unit input; validator prevents such levels from release.
 55: 
 56: ### `criticalPathLength`
 57: 
 58: Use only for monotonic levels whose logical constraints can be represented as a static dependency graph without required rotate or stateful gate sequencing.
 59: 
 60: Create blocker edges:
 61: 
 62: ```text
 63: blocker unit -> blocked unit
 64: ```
 65: 
 66: Only live units are graph nodes. Obstacles and permanently blocking gates make a level unsolvable rather than becoming removable nodes.
 67: 
 68: Compute the longest DAG path in nodes. If the graph is cyclic, the validator/solver outcome determines rejection; do not silently report a path.
 69: 
 70: ### `causalUnlockDepth`
 71: 
 72: This is the primary stateful depth metric.
 73: 
 74: #### Monotonic calculation
 75: 
 76: Use exit waves:
 77: 
 78: ```text
 79: depth := 0
 80: state := initial
 81: while incomplete:
 82:   exits := legal exits at start of wave
 83:   if empty: no finite depth
 84:   apply every start-of-wave exit in stable order
 85:   depth += 1
 86: ```
 87: 
 88: Exits that become legal during a wave belong to the next wave.
 89: 
 90: #### Stateful calculation
 91: 
 92: Use macro-state search with lexicographic objective:
 93: 
 94: ```text
 95: (rotateCount, decisionLayerCount, totalActionCount)
 96: ```
 97: 
 98: - A required rotate is one layer.
 99: - The subsequent exit wave is another layer.
100: - Exit actions that were legal together and commute share a layer.
101: 
102: Required expected results:
103: 
104: ```text
105: 8 independent units              -> 1
106: 8-unit dependency chain          -> 8
107: 2 parallel chains of length 4    -> 4
108: required rotate then exit        -> 2
109: switch opens gate for later unit -> at least one additional layer
110: ```
111: 
112: ### `averageAvailableMoves`
113: 
114: Do not enumerate all escaped subsets.
115: 
116: For monotonic levels:
117: 
118: - include the canonical exit-wave trace;
119: - include a fixed number of deterministic topological traces generated from stable seeds;
120: - recommended sample count: 32 traces;
121: - record sample count and seed.
122: 
123: At each pre-action state, count legal exits. Average across sampled states.
124: 
125: For stateful levels:
126: 
127: - use the reduced closed-state graph explored by the solver when fully explored;
128: - otherwise sample deterministic winning traces and label the metric sampled.
129: 
130: ### `forcedStateRatio`
131: 
132: Ratio of observed decision states with exactly one legal exit. A state with rotate choices but no legal exit is not counted as a one-exit state; record it through stateful metrics.
133: 
134: ### `minRotateRequired`
135: 
136: Copy from the lexicographically optimal solver result.
137: 
138: ### `deadEndRisk`
139: 
140: For monotonic exit-only levels, exact value is 0 by contract.
141: 
142: For stateful levels:
143: 
144: ```text
145: legal rotate actions from winning-reachable closed states
146: that lead to states with no winning solution
147: /
148: all legal rotate actions from those states
149: ```
150: 
151: Use exact reduced graph when available. Otherwise deterministic sampling with coverage metadata.
152: 
153: Do not count a blocked tap as a legal puzzle action.
154: 
155: ### `meaningfulDecisionProxy`
156: 
157: Count closed states containing at least two legal non-equivalent rotate actions that lead to different canonical closed successor states and different future solution consequences.
158: 
159: Independent exit ordering is not a meaningful decision because it is collapsed by exit closure.
160: 
161: ### `mechanicUsage`
162: 
163: Report whether each obstacle, gate, switch, and multi-cell unit participates in a constraint or solution transition.
164: 
165: An obstacle participates only when it blocks at least one direction relevant to a live unit or makes rotate meaningful. A decorative obstacle that affects no legal or candidate route is unused and should reject generated candidates.
166: 
167: ## Outputs
168: 
169: `npm run levels:report` writes:
170: 
171: ```text
172: reports/levels/difficulty.json
173: reports/levels/difficulty.csv
174: reports/levels/difficulty.md
175: ```
176: 
177: Metadata:
178: 
179: - tool version;
180: - git commit;
181: - level-set version;
182: - generator version;
183: - command and arguments;
184: - timestamp;
185: - exact/sampled coverage;
186: - seeds and sample counts;
187: - solver budgets.
188: 
189: The CLI fails for invalid, unsolvable, inconclusive, or unapproved out-of-band release levels.
</file>

<file path="doc/goal/implementation/05_100_LEVEL_CATALOG.md">
  1: # Stage G — Full 100-Level Catalog and Five Difficulty Phases
  2: 
  3: ## Global catalog contract
  4: 
  5: The released catalog contains exactly 100 levels with stable IDs:
  6: 
  7: ```text
  8: level-001 ... level-100
  9: ```
 10: 
 11: All 100 levels are in redesign scope. Existing level geometry may be replaced. Existing numeric IDs are not preserved in runtime types; progression position is preserved through stable string IDs.
 12: 
 13: Every level must have:
 14: 
 15: - valid static data;
 16: - a manifest;
 17: - a deterministic representative solution;
 18: - a solver result inside release budgets;
 19: - a difficulty report;
 20: - phase target compliance or an explicit approved exception;
 21: - deterministic reproduction metadata;
 22: - mobile-readable geometry.
 23: 
 24: ## Global design limits
 25: 
 26: - Recommended board range: 3×3 through 8×8.
 27: - A 9×9 board requires explicit mobile evidence and an approved exception.
 28: - No released level may use 30–50 units as a substitute for depth.
 29: - Recommended maximum unit count is 14.
 30: - Every obstacle must participate in a route constraint.
 31: - Gates and switches must be readable and causally relevant.
 32: - Every level must be completable without pixel guessing.
 33: - Timer is assigned only after puzzle metrics pass.
 34: 
 35: ## Phase target bands
 36: 
 37: These are release bands, not merely suggestions. An exception must be declared in the manifest and listed in the report.
 38: 
 39: | Metric | Phase 1 | Phase 2 | Phase 3 | Phase 4 | Phase 5 |
 40: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
 41: | Levels | 001–020 | 021–040 | 041–060 | 061–080 | 081–100 |
 42: | Board | 3–5 | 5–7 | 5–7 | 6–8 | 6–8 |
 43: | Units | 1–7 | 6–10 | 7–11 | 8–12 | 9–14 |
 44: | Initial exits | 1–3 | 1–3 | 1–3 | 1–2 | 1–3 |
 45: | Initial exit ratio | lesson-specific, <= 65% | 20–45% | 15–40% | 10–30% | 8–25% |
 46: | Critical/causal depth | 1–4 | 4–7 | 5–9 | 7–11 | 9–14 |
 47: | Average exits | 1–3.5 | 1.5–3.5 | 1–3 | 1–2.8 | 1–2.5 |
 48: | Minimum rotate | 0–1 | 0–1 | 0–1 | exactly 1 for at least 12 levels | 1–2 |
 49: | Meaningful decisions | 0–1 | 1–2 | 1–3 | 2–4 | 3–6 |
 50: | Dead-end risk | 0 | 0–5% | 0–12% | 5–25% | 8–30% |
 51: 
 52: Dead-end risk above zero requires visible signaling and a simple restart/recovery expectation in the manifest.
 53: 
 54: ## Phase 1 — Foundation (`level-001`–`level-020`)
 55: 
 56: ### Source strategy
 57: 
 58: Hand-author all 20 levels in `scripts/levels/catalog/phase1Authored.ts`.
 59: 
 60: ### Purpose
 61: 
 62: Teach and reinforce the full v1 vocabulary without noise.
 63: 
 64: ### Required sequence
 65: 
 66: | Level range | Required concept |
 67: | --- | --- |
 68: | 001–003 | clear exit, direction reading, escaped unit removal |
 69: | 004–006 | blocker order and short chains |
 70: | 007–009 | fork and join dependency patterns |
 71: | 010–012 | multi-cell leading-edge rules |
 72: | 013–014 | obstacle and rotate tutorial; rotate required in at least one |
 73: | 015–016 | basic switch crossing and one-way gate opening |
 74: | 017–018 | multi-cell plus blocker/gate reinforcement |
 75: | 019–020 | small mixed review with one meaningful choice at most |
 76: 
 77: ### Algorithm
 78: 
 79: No random generation. Author geometry deliberately, validate, solve, and analyze after every level. The phase builder may normalize data but may not invent geometry.
 80: 
 81: ### Hard constraints
 82: 
 83: - No footprint overlap.
 84: - No intentional dead end.
 85: - At most one newly introduced concept per lesson level.
 86: - Tutorial text only where a mechanic is introduced.
 87: - At least one level must prove full leading-edge checking for a 2×1 unit.
 88: - At least one level must prove full leading-edge checking for a 1×2 unit.
 89: 
 90: ## Phase 2 — Structured Dependency (`level-021`–`level-040`)
 91: 
 92: ### Source strategy
 93: 
 94: Generate from explicit monotonic dependency blueprints in `phase2Blueprints.ts`, then commit deterministic seeds and manifests.
 95: 
 96: ### Blueprint types
 97: 
 98: - `CHAIN`
 99: - `FORK`
100: - `JOIN`
101: - `PARALLEL_CHAINS`
102: - `SPINE_WITH_BRANCHES`
103: - `MULTICELL_CROSS_LANE`
104: 
105: ### Algorithm
106: 
107: 1. Select target unit count and graph template.
108: 2. Construct a DAG with target roots and longest path.
109: 3. Embed nodes in reverse topological order so later-placed units block intended earlier nodes.
110: 4. Verify geometric dependency graph matches the blueprint within declared tolerances.
111: 5. Add multi-cell units by structural role, not random chance.
112: 6. Add an obstacle only if it participates in a validated route constraint.
113: 7. Require fast-path no-rotate solvability unless the manifest explicitly marks one optional rotate lesson.
114: 8. Reject candidates outside target bands.
115: 
116: ### Hard constraints
117: 
118: - Gate/switch is optional but not required in this phase.
119: - No level relies on rotate to repair random generation.
120: - At least 12 of 20 levels must have a critical path of 5 or more.
121: - No level may begin with more than 45% of units immediately escapable.
122: 
123: ## Phase 3 — Stateful Mechanics (`level-041`–`level-060`)
124: 
125: ### Source strategy
126: 
127: Generate from explicit gate/switch causal templates in `phase3Blueprints.ts`, with authored corrections allowed.
128: 
129: ### Template types
130: 
131: - `SWITCH_OPENS_SINGLE_GATE`
132: - `SWITCH_UNLOCKS_BRANCH`
133: - `TWO_SWITCH_PARALLEL`
134: - `SHORT_GATE_CHAIN`
135: - `ROTATE_TO_SWITCH`
136: - `MULTICELL_GATE_LANE`
137: 
138: ### Algorithm
139: 
140: 1. Build a causal blueprint before geometry.
141: 2. Assign switch and target gate IDs explicitly.
142: 3. Ensure the intended exiting unit crosses the switch footprint.
143: 4. Ensure the gate blocks at least one required unit before opening.
144: 5. Embed remaining monotonic dependencies around the causal chain.
145: 6. Solve using fast closure first; invoke stateful search only when rotate is required.
146: 7. Reject unused gates, switches, or obstacles.
147: 8. Reject candidates where stateful mechanics do not increase causal depth.
148: 
149: ### Phase composition requirements
150: 
151: - At least 14 levels contain gate/switch mechanics.
152: - At least 6 levels require exactly one rotate.
153: - At least 6 levels combine a multi-cell unit with a stateful gate constraint.
154: - No toggle or closing gate.
155: 
156: ## Phase 4 — Strategic Combination (`level-061`–`level-080`)
157: 
158: ### Source strategy
159: 
160: Generate from strategic rotate-resource blueprints in `phase4Blueprints.ts`; hand-tune any accepted candidate whose visual readability is insufficient.
161: 
162: ### Template types
163: 
164: - `COMPETING_ROTATE_TARGETS`
165: - `ROTATE_BEFORE_GATE_CHAIN`
166: - `FORKED_STATEFUL_BRANCH`
167: - `MULTICELL_RESOURCE_LOCK`
168: - `SAFE_AND_DEAD_END_ROTATE`
169: - `CONVERGING_GATE_PATHS`
170: 
171: ### Algorithm
172: 
173: 1. Build two or more plausible rotate candidates.
174: 2. Guarantee at least one optimal solution with exactly one rotate.
175: 3. When an incorrect rotate creates a dead end, declare the signal and recovery in the manifest.
176: 4. Apply exit closure between rotate decisions.
177: 5. Use the stateful solver and dead-end analyzer as an acceptance gate.
178: 6. Reject candidates whose apparent choices collapse to equivalent successors.
179: 7. Reject candidates with an unreasonably high dead-end risk or unreadable state.
180: 
181: ### Phase composition requirements
182: 
183: - At least 12 levels require exactly one rotate.
184: - At least 10 levels contain two non-equivalent rotate choices.
185: - At least 10 levels use gate/switch.
186: - At least 10 levels use multi-cell units structurally.
187: 
188: ## Phase 5 — Expert Causal Planning (`level-081`–`level-100`)
189: 
190: ### Source strategy
191: 
192: Use strict constrained generation from `phase5Blueprints.ts`, followed by mandatory authored review. A level may be marked `generated-with-override` when geometry is hand-corrected while preserving its seed/template lineage.
193: 
194: ### Template types
195: 
196: - `DOUBLE_ROTATE_PLAN`
197: - `DEEP_GATE_CHAIN`
198: - `MULTI_BRANCH_RESOURCE_PLAN`
199: - `LOW_ROOT_CAUSAL_SPINE`
200: - `NONCOMMUTING_ROTATE_CHOICES`
201: - `EXPERT_MIXED_MECHANICS`
202: 
203: ### Algorithm
204: 
205: 1. Choose a causal blueprint with target depth and decision count.
206: 2. Assign one or two rotate charges explicitly.
207: 3. Embed gates, switches, and multi-cell blockers.
208: 4. Validate physical and causal roles.
209: 5. Solve within release budgets.
210: 6. Analyze exact metrics when the reduced graph is fully explored.
211: 7. Otherwise use deterministic sampled metrics but never accept an `INCONCLUSIVE` solver result.
212: 8. Reject visual density without causal participation.
213: 9. Require manual mobile readability review.
214: 
215: ### Hard constraints
216: 
217: - No new mechanic beyond v1.
218: - No level is made expert only by short timer.
219: - Initial exit ratio must remain at or below 25% unless an approved readability exception exists.
220: - At least 10 levels require two rotate decisions or one rotate with three or more meaningful choice states.
221: 
222: ## Timer assignment
223: 
224: Assign timer after a level passes logic targets:
225: 
226: ```text
227: timeLimit = observationAllowance
228:           + expectedActionCount * inputAndAnimationAllowance
229:           + mobileBuffer
230: ```
231: 
232: The generator/report must record the components used. Timer expiry does not lose the level; it removes the timer star condition.
233: 
234: ## Runtime generation output
235: 
236: `levels:generate` must:
237: 
238: 1. Build all 100 candidates from phase sources.
239: 2. Normalize entity state.
240: 3. Validate the catalog.
241: 4. Solve every level.
242: 5. Replay every solution.
243: 6. Analyze every level.
244: 7. Enforce phase bands and exceptions.
245: 8. Write reports to a temporary directory.
246: 9. Write `levels.ts.tmp`.
247: 10. Atomically promote the runtime file and reports only after all gates pass.
248: 
249: A failure must leave the previous valid `levels.ts` untouched.
</file>

<file path="doc/goal/implementation/06_PROGRESS_AND_ECONOMY_REMOVAL.md">
  1: # Stage H — Economy Removal and Progress Migration
  2: 
  3: ## Objective
  4: 
  5: Delete the economy and migrate progress to one versioned payload using stable level IDs.
  6: 
  7: ## Economy deletion checklist
  8: 
  9: Delete or rewrite every confirmed occurrence of:
 10: 
 11: - `coins` in `GameState`;
 12: - `coins` in progress snapshots;
 13: - `coinsEarnedThisLevel`;
 14: - coin sanitization;
 15: - +10 unit reward;
 16: - completion/star coin bonuses;
 17: - replay detection for rewards;
 18: - coin rollback on restart;
 19: - hint price and insufficient-coin disablement;
 20: - coin HUD metric;
 21: - win-overlay coin badge;
 22: - Dashboard coin/asset section;
 23: - localStorage coin key behavior;
 24: - economy tests and docs;
 25: - economy-only imports, CSS, and icons.
 26: 
 27: Do not leave unused economy fields set to zero. Remove the feature.
 28: 
 29: ## Current schema constants
 30: 
 31: ```ts
 32: export const TRIBE_OUT_PROGRESS_KEY = "tribeout_progress";
 33: export const PROGRESS_SCHEMA_VERSION = 2;
 34: export const LEVEL_SET_VERSION = 2;
 35: ```
 36: 
 37: The implementation may increment the numeric versions only if repository history already uses these values. The semantic contract is fixed.
 38: 
 39: ## Canonical progress payload
 40: 
 41: ```ts
 42: export interface TribeOutProgressSnapshot {
 43:   schemaVersion: number;
 44:   levelSetVersion: number;
 45:   unlockedLevelIds: LevelId[];
 46:   currentLevelId: LevelId;
 47:   starsByLevelId: Partial<Record<LevelId, StarRating>>;
 48: }
 49: ```
 50: 
 51: ## Default progress
 52: 
 53: ```text
 54: unlocked: [level-001]
 55: current: level-001
 56: stars: empty
 57: ```
 58: 
 59: ## Sanitization
 60: 
 61: - Unknown level IDs are removed.
 62: - Unlocks are normalized to a contiguous prefix ending at the highest valid unlocked catalog position.
 63: - `level-001` is always unlocked.
 64: - Current ID must be valid and unlocked; otherwise use the highest valid unlocked ID.
 65: - Stars are clamped to integers 0–3.
 66: - Stars for unknown IDs are removed.
 67: - Duplicate unlocked IDs are removed.
 68: - Invalid JSON returns default progress.
 69: - Storage exceptions return playable in-memory defaults.
 70: 
 71: ## Legacy keys
 72: 
 73: Read and remove after successful canonical persistence:
 74: 
 75: ```text
 76: tribeout_coins
 77: tribeout_highest_level
 78: tribeout_current_level
 79: tribeout_level_stars
 80: ```
 81: 
 82: Coins are discarded. They are not mapped into another resource.
 83: 
 84: ## Legacy migration
 85: 
 86: 1. Read legacy highest and current numeric indexes.
 87: 2. Clamp indexes to 0–99.
 88: 3. Convert the highest index into a contiguous unlocked ID prefix.
 89: 4. Convert current index to a valid unlocked ID.
 90: 5. Parse legacy stars only to determine that old progress existed; because all 100 levels are redesigned in this release, reset all stars.
 91: 6. Persist canonical payload.
 92: 7. Only after persistence succeeds, remove all legacy keys.
 93: 8. Return canonical progress.
 94: 
 95: ## Level-set migration
 96: 
 97: When loading an existing canonical payload with an older `levelSetVersion`:
 98: 
 99: - preserve the valid contiguous unlock prefix;
100: - preserve valid current level position;
101: - reset stars for the level IDs listed in the migration definition;
102: - for this redesign release, that list is all 100 IDs;
103: - update `levelSetVersion` exactly once;
104: - persist the migrated payload;
105: - repeated loads must not reset newly earned stars again.
106: 
107: ## Win persistence
108: 
109: On win:
110: 
111: - keep the highest stars for the completed level;
112: - unlock the next level ID if one exists;
113: - do not change current level until the user chooses Next;
114: - do not grant any currency or reward.
115: 
116: On Next:
117: 
118: - persist the next unlocked level as `currentLevelId`.
119: 
120: On replay/restart:
121: 
122: - do not alter progress except current level when explicitly navigated.
123: 
124: ## Clear progress
125: 
126: `clearTribeOutProgress` removes:
127: 
128: - canonical key;
129: - all legacy keys;
130: - any temporary migration key created by implementation.
131: 
132: It must not alter music/SFX settings unless they share no progress contract and the product explicitly requests it.
133: 
134: ## Required tests
135: 
136: - no storage returns defaults;
137: - legacy highest/current migrate to stable IDs;
138: - legacy coins are discarded;
139: - all legacy stars reset for catalog v2;
140: - unlock/current preserved and clamped;
141: - corrupt JSON;
142: - negative and huge indexes;
143: - unknown IDs;
144: - non-contiguous unlock array normalization;
145: - invalid star values;
146: - migration idempotence;
147: - second load preserves newly earned v2 stars;
148: - storage write failure returns playable data;
149: - clear removes canonical and every legacy key;
150: - reorder fixture resolves by stable ID rather than old numeric object keys.
</file>

<file path="doc/goal/implementation/07_RUNTIME_INTEGRATION.md">
  1: # Stage I — Runtime Integration
  2: 
  3: ## Objective
  4: 
  5: Integrate the new pure puzzle domain, progress schema, free hints, and 100-level catalog without regressing rendering, animation, audio, accessibility, or lifecycle safety.
  6: 
  7: ## Attempt state flow
  8: 
  9: ```text
 10: load progress
 11:   -> resolve currentLevelId
 12:   -> create initial puzzle state
 13:   -> build runtime GameState
 14:   -> render board and controls
 15: ```
 16: 
 17: ## Tap flow
 18: 
 19: ```text
 20: entity activation
 21:   -> UI confirms live unit
 22:   -> runtime adapter dispatches exit or rotate
 23:   -> domain returns accepted/rejected transition
 24:   -> runtime applies lives/status/animation/audio/persistence effects
 25: ```
 26: 
 27: ## Invalid-target behavior
 28: 
 29: For obstacle, gate, switch, escaped unit, missing ID, or non-playing state:
 30: 
 31: - no life change;
 32: - no rotate charge change;
 33: - no puzzle-state change;
 34: - no bump or escape animation;
 35: - no bump/escape audio;
 36: - no persistence write.
 37: 
 38: Direct domain and adapter calls must enforce this even if UI guards fail.
 39: 
 40: ## Blocked live unit
 41: 
 42: A blocked live-unit tap:
 43: 
 44: - returns `BLOCKED_PATH` from the domain;
 45: - subtracts one life in the runtime adapter;
 46: - records `lastBumpedEntityId`;
 47: - triggers one bump animation and sound;
 48: - loses when new lives value is zero;
 49: - does not mutate puzzle entities, switches, gates, directions, or rotate charges.
 50: 
 51: ## Successful exit
 52: 
 53: - commit the returned `PuzzleState`;
 54: - increment escaped count exactly once;
 55: - set last escaped ID;
 56: - clear bump ID;
 57: - play escape audio once unless the same transition wins;
 58: - when complete, play win audio and scenery boom once;
 59: - calculate stars once;
 60: - persist stars/unlock once.
 61: 
 62: ## Star contract
 63: 
 64: ```text
 65: 1 star: complete the level
 66: +1 star: finish without losing a life
 67: +1 star: use no hints and finish with timeRemaining > 0
 68: ```
 69: 
 70: Maximum is 3. Persist the best star rating only.
 71: 
 72: Economy removal does not change this rule.
 73: 
 74: ## Hint contract
 75: 
 76: Hints are free.
 77: 
 78: `handleHint`:
 79: 
 80: 1. requires playing status and no active hint animation;
 81: 2. obtains legal exit actions from the pure domain;
 82: 3. selects deterministically:
 83:    - preferred: first exit in the representative solver trace that is legal in current state;
 84:    - fallback: first legal exit sorted by unit ID;
 85: 4. increments `hintsUsed` once;
 86: 5. highlights the selected live unit for 1.5 seconds;
 87: 6. owns and clears its timer through `hintTimerRef`.
 88: 
 89: If no legal exit exists but a rotate is required, the hint system may highlight the rotate control and target unit only when a solver-derived legal rotate recommendation is available. If this UX is not implemented, the button must remain inert rather than giving a wrong unit hint.
 90: 
 91: ## Timer contract
 92: 
 93: - decrement once per second only while game is active, tab is visible, status is playing, and time remains positive;
 94: - stop at zero;
 95: - zero does not set lost status;
 96: - zero only prevents the timer/no-hint star bonus;
 97: - clear timeout on dependency change and unmount;
 98: - do not create duplicate intervals/timeouts in Strict Mode.
 99: 
100: ## Rotate UI contract
101: 
102: - button reads charges from `gameState.puzzle.rotateChargesRemaining`;
103: - disabled at zero or non-playing status;
104: - selected state toggles without puzzle mutation;
105: - accepted rotate clears selected tool;
106: - invalid target does not consume charge and may leave selected tool active so the player can choose a valid unit;
107: - restart restores level-configured charges.
108: 
109: ## Level navigation
110: 
111: - resolve all navigation by stable level ID and catalog maps;
112: - no persisted numeric index identity;
113: - next level must already be unlocked after win;
114: - Dashboard derives highest unlocked position from catalog order;
115: - Settings navigation does not destroy attempt state unless existing behavior explicitly remounts it;
116: - progress remains synchronized when Dashboard opens.
117: 
118: ## React/Pixi lifecycle
119: 
120: Preserve and verify:
121: 
122: - one Pixi canvas per mounted board;
123: - safe async `Application.init` disposal;
124: - no render after destroyed app;
125: - resize uses current refs;
126: - entity animation timeouts clear on effect cleanup;
127: - bump and hint timers clear on unmount;
128: - App scenery timer clears on unmount;
129: - audio listeners are not duplicated.
130: 
131: ## Accessibility
132: 
133: - only live units receive button role, focus, click, Enter, and Space activation;
134: - environmental entities receive descriptive non-button semantics;
135: - direction remains in unit aria label;
136: - board remains a named group;
137: - overlays remain modal dialogs;
138: - progress bar values remain correct;
139: - controls meet existing touch target requirements;
140: - reduced motion disables or minimizes non-essential movement without hiding state changes.
141: 
142: ## Responsive requirements
143: 
144: Re-run existing viewport audit and preserve:
145: 
146: ```text
147: 375x667
148: 390x844
149: 430x932
150: 844x390
151: 768x1024
152: 1366x768
153: 1440x900
154: ```
155: 
156: All 100 levels do not need screenshots at every viewport, but representative levels from each phase must be manually tested on mobile portrait and desktop, including the largest board and largest multi-cell footprint.
</file>

<file path="doc/goal/implementation/08_GENERATOR_ALGORITHMS.md">
  1: # Stage G Detail — Phase-Specific Generator Algorithms
  2: 
  3: ## Shared deterministic random source
  4: 
  5: Create `scripts/levels/generator/random.ts`.
  6: 
  7: Use a deterministic PRNG whose output is stable across Node versions. Do not use `Math.random`, object-key iteration order, locale sorting, or cryptographic buffer mutation as the existing CJS generator does.
  8: 
  9: Required API:
 10: 
 11: ```ts
 12: export interface RandomSource {
 13:   nextFloat(): number;
 14:   nextInt(minInclusive: number, maxExclusive: number): number;
 15:   pick<T>(values: readonly T[]): T;
 16:   shuffle<T>(values: readonly T[]): T[];
 17: }
 18: 
 19: export function createRandomSource(seed: string): RandomSource;
 20: ```
 21: 
 22: A small fixed algorithm such as `xmur3` + `mulberry32` is acceptable when implemented locally and regression-tested with fixed expected sequences.
 23: 
 24: ## Shared embedding contract
 25: 
 26: `embedBlueprint.ts` receives:
 27: 
 28: - dependency blueprint;
 29: - board dimensions;
 30: - phase constraints;
 31: - deterministic random source;
 32: - attempt budget.
 33: 
 34: It returns either a candidate geometry or a structured failure.
 35: 
 36: ### Placement state
 37: 
 38: Track:
 39: 
 40: - occupied physical cells;
 41: - entity placements;
 42: - intended blocker edges already satisfied;
 43: - unintended blocker edges introduced;
 44: - switch/gate causal links;
 45: - unit forward lanes;
 46: - mobile density estimate.
 47: 
 48: ### Reverse-topological embedding
 49: 
 50: For monotonic `blocks-exit` edges, place the blocked node first and blockers later.
 51: 
 52: For each node in reverse topological order:
 53: 
 54: 1. enumerate allowed footprint/direction placements in stable coordinate order;
 55: 2. filter out-of-bounds and overlap placements;
 56: 3. verify the node's own intended escape properties;
 57: 4. score how many required edges the placement satisfies;
 58: 5. penalize unintended edges and high visual density;
 59: 6. choose among equal best placements using deterministic random source;
 60: 7. backtrack when remaining nodes cannot be placed.
 61: 
 62: Do not accept a geometry whose measured dependency graph materially differs from the blueprint.
 63: 
 64: ## Dependency graph verification
 65: 
 66: After embedding:
 67: 
 68: 1. derive geometric blocker edges using the pure domain occupancy/path rules;
 69: 2. compare intended and actual unit dependency edges;
 70: 3. require every intended edge;
 71: 4. reject unexpected edges that change critical path, roots, or intended opening beyond manifest tolerance;
 72: 5. record harmless extra edges only when explicitly allowed by the template.
 73: 
 74: ## Gate/switch embedding
 75: 
 76: For each gate chain:
 77: 
 78: 1. place a gate on the required exit path of one or more target units;
 79: 2. prove the closed gate blocks those targets;
 80: 3. place a switch on the complete traversed route of the designated key unit;
 81: 4. prove the key unit can successfully exit before gate opening under the intended prerequisite state;
 82: 5. prove the exit activates the switch and opens the correct gate;
 83: 6. reject switches that no solution crosses;
 84: 7. reject gates that block no required unit.
 85: 
 86: ## Multi-cell roles
 87: 
 88: A multi-cell unit is allowed only with a declared role:
 89: 
 90: - blocks two lanes;
 91: - requires full leading-edge clearance;
 92: - bridges dependency branches;
 93: - makes a rotate decision spatially distinct;
 94: - interacts with a gate lane.
 95: 
 96: Reject random multi-cell decoration.
 97: 
 98: ## Obstacle roles
 99: 
100: An obstacle is accepted only when the analyzer proves at least one:
101: 
102: - blocks a candidate exit direction;
103: - makes a required rotate direction meaningful;
104: - narrows an intended lane without creating hidden ambiguity;
105: - separates two stateful branches visually and logically.
106: 
107: ## Phase 1 builder
108: 
109: No random geometry. Normalize authored input, validate, solve, and report.
110: 
111: ## Phase 2 builder
112: 
113: Use DAG blueprint generation:
114: 
115: ```text
116: select template
117: -> select unit count/depth/root count
118: -> create DAG
119: -> embed reverse-topologically
120: -> verify graph
121: -> fast-solve
122: -> analyze
123: ```
124: 
125: Rotate charges default to 0. Optional rotate levels must be explicitly authored or template-marked.
126: 
127: ## Phase 3 builder
128: 
129: Use causal chain generation:
130: 
131: ```text
132: create base DAG
133: -> inject switch/key/gate/blocked target chain
134: -> embed chain first
135: -> embed remaining DAG
136: -> verify gate participation
137: -> solve
138: -> analyze stateful depth
139: ```
140: 
141: ## Phase 4 builder
142: 
143: Use rotate-choice generation:
144: 
145: ```text
146: create at least two plausible rotate targets
147: -> reserve one optimal target
148: -> embed alternate target leading to distinct closed successor
149: -> add gate/multicell dependencies
150: -> solve all rotate branches
151: -> measure meaningful decisions/dead-end risk
152: ```
153: 
154: A visually plausible but canonically equivalent rotate choice does not satisfy the template.
155: 
156: ## Phase 5 builder
157: 
158: Use deep causal search:
159: 
160: ```text
161: create depth/decision target
162: -> select one/two rotate-resource structure
163: -> compose gate chains and branch joins
164: -> embed with backtracking
165: -> validate
166: -> solve under release budget
167: -> analyze
168: -> require authored readability review
169: ```
170: 
171: ## Candidate attempt policy
172: 
173: Each level has a deterministic attempt sequence:
174: 
175: ```text
176: seed = "<levelId>:<generatorVersion>:<attempt>"
177: ```
178: 
179: Set an explicit attempt budget per phase. Recommended:
180: 
181: - Phase 2: 500
182: - Phase 3: 1,000
183: - Phase 4: 2,000
184: - Phase 5: 5,000
185: 
186: Failure to find an acceptable candidate returns a structured generation failure. Do not reduce target depth/unit/decision requirements silently.
187: 
188: ## Authored overrides
189: 
190: An override may adjust geometry or entities after generation, but must:
191: 
192: - retain the original seed/template metadata;
193: - change `sourceKind` to `generated-with-override`;
194: - pass full validation/solve/analyze;
195: - document the reason.
196: 
197: ## Atomic promotion
198: 
199: Never write partially accepted levels into runtime output. Build the complete catalog in memory, evaluate all 100, write temporary outputs, then rename atomically.
</file>

<file path="doc/goal/quality/01_TEST_MATRIX.md">
  1: # Complete Automated Test Matrix
  2: 
